The assessment for Day 230 (21:38) is written to session_plan/assessment.md and committed as 92d59e43. To get the commit through I had to force-add the file because .gitignore excludes session_plan/. Earlier sessions committed it the same way, so it should be fine, but undo it if you don't want that file in the repo.

State: the harness's build and tests were green. In an empty directory with no config, yoyo -p ran on claude-opus-5-5, answered PONG and exited 0. The four finished evolve runs today all succeeded, with no reverts, CI failures or provider errors in the last 14 days. The trajectory says the dispatch subsystem got 5 of the last 9 self-driven changes, so this slot should go somewhere else.

The main finding, from the CI logs: each session starts by trying to update yoagent to the newest patch (0.25.6 tonight). That runs the full build and test suite, and every time one test fails: every_dependency_version_claim_names_the_pinned_yoagent_version. It requires each version note in my docs and code to name the exact locked version, so any patch release fails it and the update is rolled back. That happened in both the 14:31 and 19:18 sessions, and each attempt throws away a full run of about 10 minutes. 0.25.7 is out now too, so it will keep happening. #1039 has the fix Yuanhao already chose: the notes name only major.minor (0.25). None of the files involved are on the protected list.

Suggested priorities for the planner:
1. #1039. Before calling it fixed, check what 0.25.6 and 0.25.7 changed against my own claims: DeepSeek prices in src/format/cost.rs, and what CLAUDE.md says about MCP. Don't bump Cargo.lock in the same task; the next session's start-up update will pick up 0.25.7 by itself.
2. One false sentence in the user docs (part of #1023), at docs/src/configuration/permissions.md:212-215. It still tells people to unset RIPGREP_CONFIG_PATH to get dropped matches back. That has done nothing since yoagent 0.25.5, which stops search reading the user's ripgrep config. Docs-only, and outside dispatch.
3. Alternatives outside dispatch:
   • #1030: a typo in the cost budget setting silently turns the budget off.
   • The next provider in #1037: openai still defaults to gpt-5. Use the same three checks the anthropic fix used.

Research: Claude Code names every MCP tool mcp__<server>__<tool>, so an MCP tool can never clash with a built-in. yoyo instead skips the whole server when any of its tool names clash, which means the standard filesystem server never connects. yoagent 0.25.7 fixes the prefix option so it actually renames the tools (it was stored and ignored before). Once that version is in the lock, connecting clashing servers under a prefix is a concrete feature to build. I saved this to yopedia.
